## From bytes to agents

You started with a guessing game: what word comes next? Everything since has been about how a
machine plays that game, and what gets built around it. Zoom in and out of the whole picture; each
part links back to the chapter that explains it.

<WholePicture />

In one paragraph: text becomes **tokens** (Ch 1, 4), each token becomes a **vector** (Ch 5), and a
stack of **transformer blocks** refines those vectors, with **attention** letting each token use
earlier ones (Ch 8–10). The result is a **probability** for every possible next token (Ch 2–3),
learned by **training** that nudges millions or billions of weights to make the right tokens more
likely (Ch 6–7, 12). Generating text is a **loop** of predicting and picking (Ch 11), tuning turns
that into an **assistant** (Ch 13), and a **harness** wraps it with prompts, tools, an agent loop,
context management, and guardrails (Ch 15–19). Other kinds of models change one piece and keep the
rest: encoders read both ways, mixtures of experts use part of themselves per token, recurrent
models keep a fixed memory, vision models read patches, diffusion models paint from noise, and
reasoning models write their working first (Ch 20–25). And prompting is choosing the document the
model continues: the wording, examples, instructions, and layout all shift what comes next, often in
ways you have to test to find out (Ch 26–30).

## Check yourself

<Quiz />

<RecapCard>

- A language model predicts the next token, nothing more, and does it very well at scale.
- What looks like a chat, a memory, or an agent is a harness calling that model in loops.
- Most quirks, from made-up facts to miscounted letters, follow from those two facts.
- Other models swap one piece, like the attention mask, the MLP, or what counts as a token, and
  keep the rest of the recipe.
- A prompt is the start of a document: everything in it shifts the next token, so test it.

</RecapCard>

Thanks for reading. If something here was unclear or wrong, the sources for every claim are listed
at the bottom of each chapter.
